# 01 — Scope, Behavior Safety & Stop Criteria

<!-- GEN: Why: simplifying at the wrong time, outside the diff or past the point where behavior changes turns a cleanup into an unreviewed feature change. Kind: Mandatory — keep the purpose, adapt the structure (core/authoring-guide.md). -->

<!-- GEN: Rules S1, S2, S3, S11, S12, S13 with the workspace's real phase/diff notions, checks and protected boundaries (references/discovery-and-checks.md §2). -->

> **Workspace Convention Rule (`C11` — `EXTEND` vs `INIT`):**
> - **Existing project (`EXTEND`):** Simplify strictly toward the patterns, base classes, state management, and idioms already used in the target module. Never introduce a competing idiom or paradigm during a simplification pass.
> - **Greenfield project (`INIT`):** Follow the stack's default idioms and approved architecture profile (`design.md`).

## 1. When

| Situation | Simplify? |
|---|---|
| The phase's checks pass and the diff has a named smell | Yes, inside the changed code only — the simplicity pass of {{incremental_link}} |
| A review finding names unnecessary complexity in the phase's code | Yes, as the fix revision for that finding |
| Checks are red | No — fix behavior first ({{debugging_link}}) |
| The smell is outside the diff, or the code is about to be rewritten | No — record it in Concerns |
| Writing a new feature or fixing a bug | No — that changes behavior and follows the plan |

## 2. Scope

Only the files and lines changed in this phase:

```bash
{{diff_command}}
```
<!-- GEN: e.g. git diff --name-only <base>...HEAD -- <source globs> -->

- Inside the diff: avoidable complexity under any of the four lenses is fair game.
- Outside the diff: report it in Concerns, never touch it.
- {{scope_exceptions}} <!-- GEN: history that must not be rewritten (applied migrations), generated code; delete if none -->

## 3. Before editing

Read the callers ({{caller_lookup}}), including dynamic use (string names, reflection, configuration keys), side effects, error paths, lifecycle and the tests that cover the code.

## 4. Must stay identical

Inputs · outputs · raised errors and messages · side effects and their order · state transitions · {{lifecycle_boundaries}} · accessibility behavior · performance limits.

Existing tests pass **unchanged**; the only allowed test edit is an import path after a move.

{{stack_traps}} <!-- GEN: 3–5 traps where a "simpler" form changes behavior in this stack; delete if none found -->

## 5. One step at a time

After each simplification run (`exit 0` = `PASS`, `exit != 0` = `FAIL`):

```bash
{{check_commands}}
```

Keep each command and exit code as an evidence row. Red after a step and the cause is not obvious → revert that step.

## 6. Never

- Change a test to make a simplification pass.
- Remove validation, error mapping, authorization or data-scope checks, dependency-injection boundaries, transactions or package contracts ({{protected_examples}}).
- Leave a wrapper, alias or re-export after moving code ({{clean_code_link}} C1).
- Add a suppression directive to make a check pass.

## 7. Stop criteria

| Situation | Do instead |
|---|---|
| Behavior or a public contract would change | Stop; return to {{gate_for_changes}} |
| A workspace decision ({{decision_notation}}) or architecture port requires the abstraction | Leave it; mention it in Concerns if it looks unnecessary |
| The gain is cosmetic (renaming taste, reordering) | Leave it |
| A hot path would change without a measurement | Measure first or leave it |
| The next step (or the right depth for an Altitude fix) needs code outside the diff | Record it in Concerns |
| Checks turn red and the cause is not obvious | Revert the last step |
| No named smell remains | Stop and report |

